/** Siman 334 mt_garbage editorial retranslations — ENGLISH only replacements. */
export const BLOCKS = {
  // ─── baer-heitev ───
  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1|ב":
    `Thirty. Beit Yosef wrote in the name of the Kontresim that nidui: if a sage placed him in nidui for his honor and he went away, they do not release him until after thirty days—for them; but for us, seven days, since once he went away presumably he is pleased that they release him after that time—end of his words. And the meaning is that one should not explain that bet din has no authority to reduce nidui to less than thirty days, for that is not so, as below seif 6. Shach:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|2|ה":
    `The sandal. Likewise in Orach Chayyim siman 554 seif 17 and below siman 382 seif 4. Shach wrote: I wonder—for in Beit Yosef he ruled here that wearing a sandal is permitted, and likewise Rivash siman 173 in the name of Rashba, and so is the practice; also one need not tear, wrap the head, or invert the bed like a mourner; and permitted in bathing, anointing, use of the bed, tefillin, and greeting—so agreed most poskim—end of his words:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|19|_":
    `Intended. In Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of the Ramach he explained that this is when he did not disgrace him at all but only does not honor him as he is obligated to honor him with standing and deference—his nidui is not nidui. Maharik in the name of Maharam explained that it deals with one who placed him in nidui because he withholds his money unlawfully—even though as halachah he placed him in nidui, his nidui is not nidui; and likewise Rosh in responsum—end of Shach:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|20|א":
    `To his city. Shach wrote that Bach explained that this is a menudeh for honor whom he declared cherem against them even though he did not declare cherem in bet din; but a menudeh to bet din because he did not obey judgment or cherem of bet din for a transgression—his law is equal whether in his city or another city, for to all Israel he is a menudeh and they are obligated to treat him with nidui; and likewise the Levush—end of his words:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|20|ב":
    `Like it. But not to a superior city, as Beit Yosef wrote:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|34|_":
    `Ten. Shach wrote that from Bach's words it appears the same applies above seif 33 regarding a Torah scholar who cannot release himself—ten must release him—see there—end of his words:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|43|ב":
    `Calculating. Shach wrote: it appears this is specifically when he calculates and fixes according to what they were accustomed to fix in the Land when the Temple stood; but to calculate tekufot and mazalot as practiced nowadays—on the contrary, it is a mitzvah, as they say: whoever knows to calculate tekufot and mazalot and does not calculate—regarding him Scripture says "and they do not look upon the work of Hashem" etc.:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|43|ג":
    `Who. And if he comes to bet din to complain that they placed him in nidui improperly, bet din places him in nidui—likewise the Levush; and it implies that whenever he did not come to bet din to complain, bet din does not attend to place him in nidui. In Kesef Mishneh he wrote in the name of Rashbatz in responsum that here it deals with one who is accustomed to this, that bet din attends to place him in nidui; and it implies that if he is accustomed, even if he does not complain, bet din attends to place him in nidui—end of Shach:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|44|_":
    `Question. And see above siman 228 seif 20 and siman 238 seif 20; and it is difficult, for the Mechaber's words appear contradictory—see there and it is simple. And it seems to me to resolve: what the Mechaber wrote here, that any bet din that attends to a baraita question is liable for nidui—this deals with a matter that has not yet been practiced as forbidden, in days when it was practiced as permitted, as above siman 228; and so it is in the Gemara. But afterward, even if he transgressed intentionally, they ask about him. And all this is in a matter where it is fit to practice prohibition; but in a matter that does not apply, such as one who swore not to eat bread and transgressed and ate—they release him immediately; and from this deals siman 228 and 238; and likewise this distinction is explained explicitly in siman 228 seif 5—see there:`,

  "siman_334/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|47|_":
    `Witnesses. And sometimes they need not kosher witnesses, but even a woman or minor or relatives are believed in a matter of quarrels—likewise Maharik; and likewise the rabbi wrote in Choshen Mishpat siman 37—end of Shach:`,

  // ─── beer-hagolah ───
  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|4|_":
    `From the implication of the Gemara there, daf 14b:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5|_":
    `Statement of Rav Chisda there, daf 16a:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6|_":
    `Rosh in the name of Raavad, from the incident of R. Eliezer whom they blessed and R. Akiva sat for him etc., Bava Metzia daf 59b:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|9|_":
    `Wording of Rambam in chapter 7 of Hilchot Talmud Torah; and likewise Tur and Hagahot there in the name of Rokeach; and he gave a reason that if they include him, where is their curse and what did their enactment accomplish—and he is not fit for inclusion etc. (°) See what is written at the end of the siman:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|10|_":
    `Baraita Moed Katan daf 15a:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|12|_":
    `Tur from the words of his father Rosh there, from what they asked there in the Gemara whether one is permitted in greeting etc.:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|14|_":
    `Baraita Moed Katan daf 15a, and like R. Yehuda:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|30|_":
    `There in the Gemara: if in matters of Heaven there is no wisdom and no understanding etc.; and as below seif 17:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|34|_":
    `Wording of Rambam in chapter 7 of Hilchot Talmud Torah, from what is written above in seif 15:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|37|_":
    `Baraita in the Bavli, daf 16a:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|40|_":
    `Rambam in chapter 7 of Hilchot Talmud Torah, and likewise Tur:`,

  "siman_334/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|42|_":
    `Likewise there, from the incident of Ravina etc., Nedarim daf 8b; and from the incident of R. Eliezer etc., the vow was released, Sanhedrin daf 68a—learn from this that nidui is a vow:`,

  // ─── beur-hagra ───
  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|א":
    `And some say also members of his household. From the above (Be'er HaGolah s.k. 7) that likewise members of the household. And as he wrote the reason that his wife and sons are permitted because his sustenance they did not drive away, as written in Nedarim 39a; and these are the words of Rosh and Tur; and the first reason is the words of Rashba and Ran from the sugya in Nedarim 7, as written [but in Moed Katan 17a the version is in his lodging] and Raavan there. (Likkut) Except etc. and some say etc. Raavan in Nedarim 7b s.v. "and when he enters" etc., and in Rosh there (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ב":
    `And one does not eat etc. As written there 16a, and that one eats and drinks etc.—it implies that nevertheless there is prohibition otherwise:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ד":
    `(Likkut) And forbidden in laundry and haircut. Gemara and baraita there; but other matters that are raised in a doubt—we hold rabbinic doubt to be lenient. Rif; and Rosh wrote in the name of Raavad: it is difficult—for if so, that which is taught "and so you find" etc.—to what does it refer? And it appears necessarily that wearing the sandal is meant, as we find regarding R. Eliezer; and it is taught in the Tosefta (in our Tosefta it is not found except in great mourning ch. 5) and Yerushalmi (ch. 2 of Berachot, and learn from this) "but a mourner and menudeh" etc.; and as explained above siman 382 seif 4; and in Rosh there:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ה":
    `And likewise forbidden to benefit from him. Likewise Rosh in the name of Raavad, and see there siman 55 (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ו":
    `And permitted to speak etc. In the beginning of chapter 2 of Derech Eretz: "one who dwells in a house" etc., and Be'er HaGolah. (Likkut) And permitted to speak etc.—for Rav Yehuda spoke with that one there 17a. Rosh there (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|_":
    `Even if they placed him in nidui etc. From what is written 17a "they brought him into the cave" etc. There:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5|_":
    `Even though etc. From what is written (16a) "and one eats and drinks" etc.—it implies, but presumably not:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6|א":
    `If they wish etc. Raavad from Yerushalmi, and in Rosh siman 55:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7|א":
    `When the time arrives etc. There from the Yerushalmi cited, and from what is written in the Gemara: nidui is not less than thirty days—and this is without Torah, as written in seif 13; and in Rosh siman 54:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|13|א":
    `This etc. and nevertheless etc. There siman 54, from the words of Rif who ruled that we do not hold like Abbaye there, from the sugya in Nedarim 7b. (Likkut) This that we say etc. and nevertheless etc., and some disagree etc., and within thirty etc. Raavan in Nedarim 7b s.v. "and learn there is no difference" etc. (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|13|ב":
    `And even if etc. As the second answer there. (Likkut) And even if he did not repent etc. So explained Raavad there for one explanation; and likewise in the Gemara in Shevuot explicitly 31a; and as written in Choshen Mishpat siman 87 seif 9; and that which we place him in nidui because he treats cherem lightly, as written in chapter 5 of Yevamot (81a) and chapter 1 of Kiddushin—except that there it implies even if he does not request release; and likewise there siman 98 seif 5; and even though they said there ninety days—the nidui is for the future that he come to bet din and pay or set a time, and every hour he is liable in nidui—unlike nidui for the past; and like that case in Shevuot that nidui is not because of payment, as written "let him be seized in laundry" etc., but because he did not wish to swear; but from R. Eliezer (Sanhedrin 68a) and Akavya (ch. 5 of Eduyot) it implies specifically when he requests; and likewise in Yerushalmi chapter 3 of Moed Katan, and they brought Rosh there "the menudeh whom they released" etc., "but in what do we stand" etc.—except that there it implies they do not release him at all before thirty days, and we do not hold thus, as written (Moed Katan 16a, and in every matter according to Rif) "totally forbidden" etc. (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|13|ד":
    `And there is etc. From what is written 14b "he goes and appeases him" etc.; and in Yerushalmi there, and they brought Rosh there in the beginning of the chapter "the menudeh" etc., "but in what do we stand" etc.; and this is what is written "and within" etc.:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|20|א":
    `But a menudeh etc. As written "a disciple to a rabbi is not a menudeh" etc.; and likewise here, for we include them in the baraita; and from this we learn what is written above "even if greater" etc.:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|23|_":
    `How etc. From what is written in Sanhedrin 68a "the vow was released," and like a vow siman 228 (seif 3)—see there:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|24|א":
    `They release etc. So implied in Nedarim 8b, and it is obvious that it is not stricter than a vow; and as written, and Raavan there:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|24|ג":
    `And every cherem etc. From the incident of R. Eliezer cited, and in Moed Katan 17a, and learn from this:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|25|_":
    `And there is etc. From what is written "an important person" etc., and "what is a maidservant" etc., and the incident of R. Eliezer; and in Rosh siman 53 and 58:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|26|ב":
    `But etc. For it is written there in the Gemara "and learn from this" only one releases his portion. There:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|26|ג":
    `And if there were etc. From the above "three who placed" etc.; and likewise for more:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|27|_":
    `They placed him in nidui without specification etc. As above siman 54 in the Hagahah. (If he treated lightly etc.—see above siman 228 s.k. 4 and s.k. 5):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|38|_":
    `In another matter etc. As written in Yevamot 121a. (Likkut) In another matter etc., and likewise in the twenty-four for which one is liable for nidui, as written in Yevamot 121a "I am mistaken" etc. (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|43|א":
    `For twenty-four matters etc. Wording of Gemara in the beginning of chapter 3 of Berachot (19a); and the Rambam cited them from scattered places in Bavli and Yerushalmi; and Raavad and Kontresim challenged that we find many more, as written in Semag and Beit Yosef—that all those are included in the fourth "one who treats lightly" etc.; and his words are astounding—for if so the Rambam should have omitted many of the twenty-four as well. (Likkut) For twenty-four etc. Tosafot Kiddushin 28a s.v. "one who calls" etc., and Ran in Nedarim 7b s.v. "and if not" etc., "for behold" etc. (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|43|ב":
    `And one need not for a matter etc. As written (Moed Katan 17a) "a Torah scholar renders judgment" etc.; and in Rosh there; and we say (Bava Kamma 112b) "an agent of bet din" etc.:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|44|_":
    `(Likkut) And likewise a Torah scholar who holds etc. Beit Yosef resolved Rambam's words that he did not count one who is included among those who disgrace a sage—and his words are astounding, for he did not examine the beginning of chapter 3 of Berachot (for if so, what is the question there and in the mishnah it is not found? "But there is" etc., and "and it is taught" etc.—has he not already counted there one who counts after the bed of Torah scholars?) (end):`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|46|א":
    `Reuven etc. There according to Rosh's explanation; and see Hagahot there:`,

  "siman_334/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|48|ב":
    `Nevertheless one is not obligated etc. Kiddushin 41a (see above siman 157 s.k. 5):`,

  // ─── pitchei-teshuva ───
  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_":
    `And even if there is concern etc. Be'er HaTev; and see responsum Rashbatz part 2 siman 47 that his view is also like Nekudot HaKesef; and likewise wrote in responsum Shemesh Tzedakah part 14 siman 48—see there; and in responsum Chavot Yair siman 141—see there; and in She'elat Yaavetz part 1 siman 79 at the end; and R. Chaim Kohen in responsum Radbaz HaChadashot siman 187, and he concluded: nevertheless the leader of the generation must be moderate in such matters, for not all people are equal and not all transgressions are equal, and all his deeds should be for the sake of Heaven—see there. [And likewise raised in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 322, and wrote there: however there is room to question where there is concern he will change his religion and take his small children with him—what is the law regarding concern for the children who did not sin? His view is that one should distinguish: one who has false beliefs—it is nearly certain he will teach his children afterward, and then the children too are included in "all who enter shall not return"; one need not be concerned for the children. And Mahari Tzahalon wrote: it seems the curse of those who believe in Shabtai Tzvi—may his name be erased—if Heaven forbid their heart does not turn to good, they pursue them with their sons and daughters etc.; but a rebel and criminal in general—who can tell us not to be concerned for his children etc.; and he expanded on this and concluded: still the matter requires examination and moderation—see there]:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|2|_":
    `And in wearing the sandal. Be'er HaTev; and what is written permitted in tefillin [see in Sefer Tiferet L'Moshe who wrote on this, and these are his words: and likewise implied from R. Eliezer ben Horkenus (beginning of chapter 4 of deaths) who laid tefillin, as he said that Horkenus his son came to remove tefillin even though he was distant—and unlike what is written in Orach Chayyim siman 38 that a menudeh and metzora are forbidden to lay tefillin—it must be said there is a scribal error. And also in chapter "These may shave" they ask: a menudeh—what is his law regarding tefillin? And it remains in doubt whether he is liable—end of his words]. And see on this in responsum Shemesh Tzedakah part 14 siman 3:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|3|_":
    `That they not circumcise his sons. R. Yosef wrote in the name of Sefer Makom Shmuel that Maharam in Hagahah comes to teach that even though his father himself was a mohel, they can decree upon him that he not circumcise his sons himself; and he himself wrote on this that this law is not clear—for perhaps they do not decree upon him as they do not decree that he not pray, read Shema, or lay tefillin; and there is no proof from what the rabbi wrote in Kenesset HaGedolah, who learned from Ramban's responsum that they can decree he not say Kaddish for his father—for there it is similar to zimmun and prayer with ten, since he needs others to fulfill the mitzvah—three for zimmun and ten for Kaddish and Kedushah—they decree upon him that he be separated and excluded and have no portion and inclusion in Israel; but a matter he can fulfill himself without any inclusion of others at all, such as circumcising his son—this they may not decree upon him—see there:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|5|_":
    `Permitted. See in responsum Chinuch Beit Yehudah siman 77 in the name of his ancestor z"l: one who was under cherem, a son was born to him before the cherem; and because he was ill his circumcision was postponed until he recovers; and meanwhile the father was caught in nidui and bet din placed him under cherem not to circumcise his son; and a certain sage wrote there that specifically before the son was born they may intensify upon the father not to circumcise his sons, for the obligation had not yet taken effect; but not after the son was born; and likewise if they decreed he not be buried when dead—specifically when he was still alive at the time of the cherem; and he z"l disagreed, and his view is that specifically regarding the dead there is a distinction whether he died before nidui or after; but regarding circumcision there is no distinction—see there in the Hagahah of his grandson the publisher of that book, who wrote that perhaps specifically in that case they decreed thus, since from Heaven they withheld him—that he was ill and his circumcision was postponed; therefore we say since it was postponed it was postponed; but to postpone circumcision from the eighth day—not—see there:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|6|_":
    `But not to sages. Be'er HaTev in the name of Shach who disagrees on this; and it is difficult—for if so, why teach "menudeh to a rabbi, menudeh to a disciple"—why specify a disciple, behold to all sages he is a menudeh? And I found in responsum Radbaz HaChadashot siman 378 that he too z"l raised "menudeh to a rabbi, menudeh to all sages," and wrote that what we teach "menudeh to a disciple" means even if the disciple became wise and greater than his rabbi—see there. And from this "menudeh to a disciple, not menudeh to rabbi"—likewise it deals even if the disciple is greater than his rabbi; and it requires study:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|11|_":
    `For he has no release. See in responsum Rashbash siman 396: a community that was placed in nidui in a dream—who releases them from nidui, from the community themselves or from another community? He raised that the menudeh can perhaps release others from his nidui; therefore in this case they should take ten of the best of the congregation at once; and even according to Rambam and Rosh z"l (there are those who disagree—as above siman 234 seif 29) that one who annuls has only two wives specifically regarding his wives because it is written "he annuls her" and not "her and her companion"—but regarding an ordinary person, many annul at once. And after those ten release the community, ten of the others should stand and release those who released the community; and this is if there are twenty who give and receive; and if there are not twenty, they release to the ten who released the community one by one—that one of the ten withdraw and the tenth joins with the other nine remaining; and they release that one; and afterward one withdraws and the one they released enters; and so until they release all of them—see there:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|16|_":
    `Matters of scribes. Be'er HaGolah; and see in responsum Avnei Nezer Panim Me'irrot part 1 siman 90; and see there what he wrote that one who became liable for nidui and wishes to pay a fine is not exempt from nidui according to law; nevertheless everything is according to what the eyes of bet din see—if they see he repents with a whole heart, it is fit to be lenient, for they are not obligated to place him in nidui publicly on this—see there. Accordingly it appears that if it was a matter where they are obligated to place him in nidui publicly, such as in seif 47, it is impossible to be lenient upon him:`,

  "siman_334/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|18|_":
    `One who calculates years. Be'er HaGolah; and see above siman 246 s.k. 4 in the name of responsum Chavot Yair:`,

  // ─── rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd ───
  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|2|_":
    `(Taz s.k. 1) And see what is written in this seif. This proof is found in Binyamin Zeev (siman 287 and siman 9) and in Chavot Yair (siman 141); he raised it as the essential view like Rama, and to this agreed in Shemesh Tzedakah (siman 68):`,

  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|3|_":
    `(Shulchan Aruch seif 2) And forbidden in laundry. In Rashbatz (part 2 siman 72) he wrote that those are only from words of scribes; but distancing four amot—this is the essence of nidui; and there is a practical difference in their doubt—see there:`,

  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|4|_":
    `(Seif 6) To reduce nidui from thirty days. And likewise to reduce in his prohibitions that are the particulars mentioned in this siman in law 2. Rekanati. And its source is in Beit Yosef, and these are his words: it is written in Kontresim in the name of Raavad that what they forbade in laws of a menudeh—that one may not sit with him, eat with him, speak with him, launder, or wear shoes—is only for an unspecified menudeh, or when they said "thus shall he be in nidui except for such-and-such a matter and such-and-such people," for what they excluded from nidui is permitted, and for all other matters he is a full menudeh; but if they specified his nidui, everything depends on their explanation—if they explained forbidden for this and that matter, for such-and-such people, and for such-and-such days—the menudeh has only what they specified and for the time they specified—end of his words; and even though the author of Kontresim wondered—from where do we have that there is authority to be lenient upon him—we have only Raavad's words z"l—end of Beit Yosef; and I wonder why the Mechaber omitted in our pure table in this seif this matter that they can be lenient upon him in his nidui law:`,

  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|5|_":
    `(Shach s.k. 20) It requires study, for behold etc. See in Avodat HaKodesh (siman 55):`,

  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|6|_":
    `(Seif 18 in Hagahah) Responsum of Rashba siman 400. See in Rivash (siman 400) and in Rashach (part 4 siman 6):`,

  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|7|_":
    `(Shach s.k. 46) According to all there is no permission to release him. It seems to me Shach's intent: for Rosh there first brings the reason that they do not release him except in his presence, for it is fit to honor him to release in his presence; and according to this reason they released him in his presence—in advance it was released; and afterward he wrote: some say the reason is because of suspicion; and accordingly even b'dieved it is not permitted—see there; and Shach holds that for the first reason, because of his honor, notification does not help; and for this Shach wrote that according to all—that is, for the second reason because of suspicion as well—there is no permission to release through notification; and it seems so from that Rosh cited the second view regarding b'dieved and the second view stringently; and he did not cite a difference that the first reason is stringently that even through notification they do not release—necessarily that even for the second reason they do not release through notification; and this is correct with Hashem's help; and nevertheless regarding b'dieved there is no proof from Rosh; and one can say that through notification b'dieved helps; however one can say: since if they had not notified afterward it would not help b'dieved the release—one can say that since at the time of release not in his presence the release did not take effect, again the correction of notification afterward does not help; and similar to what Tosafot Gittin (daf 33b) s.v. "to scatter" etc.—examine:`,

  "siman_334/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|8|_":
    `(Shulchan Aruch seif 37) Obligated to place him in nidui. In Rama (siman 332 ot 4) he wrote that now that they are accustomed to this, it is like one who says permitted, and he is included among unintentional—(and from this comes the practice not to place him in nidui—Semag siman Rama); and nevertheless one who is stringent to place him in nidui and releases him immediately—it is good:`,

  // ─── siftei-kohen ───
  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|א":
    `[Regarding a forbidden matter etc. See above seif 43 whether they place in nidui for a rabbinic prohibition:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|2|ז":
    `[And in wearing the sandal etc. And likewise in Orach Chayyim siman 554 seif 17 and below siman 382 seif 4; and I wonder—for in Beit Yosef he ruled here that wearing a sandal is permitted, and likewise Rivash siman 173 in the name of Rashba, and so is the practice:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|6|א":
    `[And bet din has authority etc. And this is not because of "one who swears to annul a mitzvah"—for to fence a matter, bet din may enact to uproot a matter of Torah:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|10|_":
    `That he not invite with three. It requires study—for in zimmun and prayer with ten in any case every menudeh is forbidden, as above seif 2; and Ramban who wrote thus—it is possible he holds that a menudeh is not forbidden in zimmun and inclusion of ten, but the authority is in the hands of bet din to intensify upon him; and according to what is written above that specifically when placed in nidui for a transgression he is not included in zimmun and ten (as explained) [as written] in the seif after this—but not when placed in nidui for monetary matters—one can say the Mechaber informs us that when placed in nidui for monetary matters, the authority is in the hands of bet din to place in nidui that he not invite with three and not pray with ten:`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|13|ב":
    `[If the nidui was for monetary matters etc. In Tur it is found (in the wording of Raavad) "or for a transgression" etc.; and it seems he is not precise—for if so, how did he write afterward "and even if he did not repent they release him after thirty"—how can it be to release him if he did not repent from the transgression? And likewise explained at the beginning of the siman that if he did not repent they wait for him another thirty days etc.; and it is forced to say that there it deals when he does not come to bet din and request they release him; and likewise in Raavad's own wording in Asheri he did not write "or for a transgression" but wrote that in monetary matters they release him after thirty even if he did not appease his litigant; and so in Rabbeinu Yerucham nidui; and it seems this "and even if he did not repent" does not refer to a transgression matter; and it appears the Mechaber omitted this "or for a transgression":]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|15|א":
    `[But he is not a menudeh to the rest of the sages. Even if they are younger than him. Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe; and see s.k. 30 what is written on this:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|15|ב":
    `[But he is a menudeh to the rest of the people who are not sages etc. This is the implication of Beit Yosef, and it is from Rambam's words; but in truth the plain sense of the Gemara and poskim implies that specifically to the rabbi he is not a menudeh, but to the rest of the world he is a menudeh even to sages like him; and likewise Rav Chisdai and the author of Kontresim and Rivash that he is a menudeh even to sages like him—all who are not greater than him—and he brings them in Beit Yosef; except that he wrote that from Rambam's words who wrote "every person" it implies he is not a menudeh except to the rest of the people and not to disciples even if younger than him, since they too are disciples; and regarding practice we have no authority to be more stringent than what is implied from Rambam's words—end of his words; and in my humble opinion it is not worthwhile with this precision to explain "all the people" and not sages, since the plain sense of the Gemara and poskim does not imply thus, and those rabbis above wrote explicitly that even sages like him are obligated to treat him with nidui; and also Tur wrote that his nidui is nidui to every other person; and so implied in the Gemara chapter Elu Megalchin beginning (Moed Katan daf 17) "menudeh to a disciple, not menudeh to a rabbi—to the rabbi he is not a menudeh, but to everyone he is a menudeh" etc.—it implies to everyone he is a menudeh even to sages like him, aside from his rabbi; and likewise Rashi daf 16 end of 1a "not a menudeh to a rabbi, but to everyone a menudeh"; and likewise R. Yerucham nidui ch. 1 "not a menudeh to a rabbi, but to all the world a menudeh"; and it appears Rambam's view is also thus; and accordingly all the more a rabbi who placed in nidui is a menudeh to the rest of the sages; however it appears [specifically] when the disciple is a Torah scholar fit to be placed in nidui for his honor, as below seif 18:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|15|ד":
    `[And his disciples cannot uphold his decrees etc. Likewise the rabbi also above siman 228 regarding a communal decree:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|19|א":
    `[An elder who placed in nidui etc. That is, a Torah scholar who placed in nidui—even though he placed in nidui as halachah, for one who does so is liable for nidui—nevertheless since he did not intend for honor of Torah, it is not nidui; and as the rabbi explains and proceeds:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|19|ב":
    `[Explanation: he did not intend etc. And in Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of Ramach he explained that this is when he did not disgrace him at all but only does not honor him as he is obligated to honor him with standing and deference—his nidui is not nidui; and Maharik in the name of Maharam explained that it deals with one who placed him in nidui before his rabbi (as Raavad and Mechaber wrote above seif 16); and he further resolved that it deals with one who placed him in nidui because he withholds his money unlawfully—even though as halachah he placed him in nidui, his nidui is not nidui; and likewise Rosh in responsum; and see above siman 66:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|20|א":
    `[Menudeh to his city etc. Bach explained that this is a menudeh for honor whom he declared cherem against them even though he did not declare cherem in bet din; but a menudeh to bet din because he did not obey judgment or cherem of bet din [or] for a transgression—his law is equal whether in his city or another city, for to all Israel he is a menudeh and they are obligated to treat him with nidui; and likewise the Atzei Chayyim:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|20|ב":
    `[To the rest of the towns like it. But not to a superior city, as Beit Yosef wrote:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|31|_":
    `Even if he set a time etc. Even though the time has not arrived—for they did not say one cannot release him until it takes effect, except regarding a vow; but nidui is lighter regarding release, for the one who placed it releases himself (as below seif 33)—likewise Rosh in responsum and Atzei Chayyim and Beit Yosef that even if others placed him in nidui, the law is thus; so ruled Bach; and I wonder—for above siman 228 seif 17 the Mechaber, Atzei Chayyim, and Bach ruled the same, that likewise one who places nidui upon himself conditionally—they do not release him until it takes effect, like a vow; and this is Rashba's view; and likewise in Bach siman 228 he wrote according to Rashba and Rosh wrote in responsum that conditional nidui one can release even though it has not taken effect—end of his words; and it requires study; and regarding halachah, even though Beit Yosef and Bach ruled here like Rosh and not like Mordechai chapter HaUmanin—nevertheless the matters appear that Rashba's words were omitted; and also that they themselves ruled in siman 228 like Rashba; therefore from their words there is no decision, and we return to the general rule of individual versus many—the halachah follows the many; and Rosh is an individual against Rashba and Mordechai (and he is in Rivash siman 472); and likewise from Rivash's words that Beit Yosef brought in siman 228 daf 268 1a, who wrote on one who accepted upon himself in cherem to do such-and-such within a year—behold this is like one who swore from now until such-and-such time he will do such-and-such—from now it takes effect until the time passes etc.; and likewise proven explicitly from his words in responsum siman 285 and siman 282, who wrote what you were uncertain about in release of cherem is because the time of payment has not yet arrived at the time of cherem release, and they do not release the vow until it takes effect; and the same for cherem and oath—there is no concern that this is said only regarding an individual who vows, but in communal enactment cherem not so; and behold as if they explicitly stipulated thus at the time of vow and cherem etc.; and likewise is essential:`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|39|א":
    `[Even though he is not ordained. And in Maharik root 170 he tends to say that even if he is not as great as him—see there:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|39|ב":
    `[And the other is not a Torah scholar. Even though he never learned before the one who placed in nidui. Maharik there. And at first glance it is difficult—for behold Maharik wrote there that this is specifically a student who needs the one placing nidui to place him in nidui; but for Rambam who holds bet din attends to place him in nidui—whether Torah scholar or am ha'aretz—since this one is not liable for nidui, bet din is obligated to place the one who placed nidui in nidui—end of his words; and he brings it in Beit Yosef; and if so, below in seif 43 the Mechaber copied Rambam's wording; and it is possible the Mechaber disagrees with Maharik in this, but holds as Ramach explained Rambam's words below—that it deals with nidui to a Torah scholar specifically; and he brings it in Beit Yosef; or he holds like Rashbatz below s.k. 86, and here it deals with one not accustomed to this:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|39|ג":
    `[And likewise a disciple etc. Maharik wrote there that from the words of Maharam's responsum it appears that even if the rabbi sinned and placed in nidui in a matter where there is room to fear perhaps he is not liable for nidui—if the disciple said "on the contrary, his nidui is nidui"—and he brings it in Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe; and see in Maharik himself who expanded on this:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|40|א":
    `[Torah scholar etc. In Rosh's responsum it is found "Torah scholar or judge" etc.; and it appears it deals with nidui placed by the judge as agent of bet din:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|40|ב":
    `[Until they placed him in nidui. And it deals that they placed him in nidui as law; and likewise Atzei Chayyim reads "he disgraced the wicked one until they placed him in nidui"; and likewise in Rosh's responsum it implies initially that he disgraced the sage until for that reason they placed him in nidui, and after they placed him in nidui he said to him "wicked one, on the contrary"—and if so certainly they placed him in nidui as law, for behold they place in nidui one who disgraces a sage, as below in Semag:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|43|ג":
    `[One who treats lightly one matter of words of scribes. Ran challenged: in some places we say that one who transgresses rabbinic matters—they flog him but do not place him in cherem; and he resolved: in a matter whose essence is rabbinic—for when he uproots it he uproots the entire mitzvah—they place him in cherem; but in a matter whose essence is from Torah and he transgresses what the sages forbade—they strike him with makat mardut; and he wrote on this in Beit Yosef, and these are his words: and it is simple—for Nittai, its essence is not from Torah unless through asmakhta; therefore it seems to me that one who challenges a matter of scribes is stricter than one who transgresses their words; and therefore even though the transgressor is liable only for makat mardut, the challenger or one who treats lightly is liable for nidui—end of his words; and I do not know what the difficulty is—for on the contrary, Nittai, since its essence is not from Torah but through asmakhta—they place him in nidui, for when he uproots it he uproots the entire mitzvah; and likewise Ran chapter Makom SheNahagu explicitly, and these are his words: and one can say that in a matter whose essence is rabbinic—for when he uproots it he uproots the entire mitzvah—they place him in cherem, like this; and as we also say "whom did they place in nidui? R. Eliezer ben Hyrkanus who challenged Nittai" etc.; and on his answer still Ran's question is difficult—why do they place in cherem one who profanes the two days of exile festivals and one who does melacha on erev Pesach after midday, and likewise one who sees a dead person and does not escort him—below siman 343; and if so it must be like Ran:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|43|ק":
    `[One who places in nidui on one not liable for nidui. And if he comes to bet din to complain that they placed him in nidui improperly, bet din places him in nidui—end of Atzei Chayyim; and it is from Beit Yosef; and it implies that whenever he did not come to bet din to complain, bet din does not attend to place him in nidui; and likewise in Magen Avraham s.p. 87 of Hilchot Talmud Torah; and in Beit Yosef it is written that Rashbatz in responsum resolved in the name of R. Shem Tov that here it deals with one who is accustomed to this that bet din attends to place him in nidui—it implies that if he is accustomed, even if he does not complain, bet din attends to place him in nidui:]`,

  "siman_334/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|44|ב":
    `[And likewise one etc. See above siman 228 in Beit Yosef that all those mentioned in this seif are included in the matters mentioned in the previous seif—that he is included among one who treats lightly one matter of words of scribes etc.—see there; and one should not challenge—if so, why did he count eleven who profane the two days of exile festivals and eleven who do melacha on erev Pesach? And one can say that it comes to teach that in those, even though there is only custom, they place him in nidui; and nevertheless according to what is written in the name of Beit Yosef above s.k. 70, "one who treats lightly" specifically means one who challenges—it is difficult that those are not included; and also according to Ran's explanation it requires study; and one can resolve with difficulty, and it requires study:]`,

  // ─── turei-zahav ───
  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|_":
    `And even if there is concern that through this he will go to bad culture. I greatly wonder at this matter—how can we be concerned for a prohibition that this one does and bring him to bad culture Heaven forbid, "all who enter shall not return"? And behold the source of this law is in Piskei Maharai siman 138, and these are his words: and proof from what I found in a collection that proved from this that is said in the group chapter HaNosei that Rabbenu HaKadosh at the time of his passing said in the way of prophecy regarding Rav Acha bar Yaashiyah that they placed him in cherem on those who stopped fruits in a basket and profaned Shabbat—and it is difficult: what does the Gemara teach us in this matter, and Rabbenu HaKadosh himself—for what halachah did he prophesy this thing? Rather to inform us that even if it appears to the sages that because they place in nidui on whomever, that because of this he will go to bad culture—they should not refrain from doing so according to halachah—end of his words. And what he wrote in the name of chapter HaNosei is a scribal error; rather this prophecy of Rabbi is in chapter Ten Yuchsin (Kiddushin daf 72b); and in my humble opinion from this there is no proof at all—for there it appears Rabbenu HaKadosh came to warn the listeners that they should know there are disqualified people in Babylon, as he said there "there is a place where all are mamzerim" etc.; and on this he also said there is a place in Babylon that turned today from following the Place—blessed be He—that they placed Rav Acha in cherem etc.; and therefore one should distance from them. And I say to you: all the more a kal va'chomer—for his intent in prophecy was to prove the other things he said, even though they were not clear to the listeners; and they bring proof from this thing that they see he knows in the Land of Israel what was done today in Babylon regarding those whom Rav Acha placed in cherem—and this is impossible except through prophecy; from this they knew that also in the other matters the spirit of Hashem spoke in him—as explained clearly the intent of this prophecy; and I have proof to the contrary from Talmud Bavli chapter 1 of Kiddushin (daf 20) that one sold for idol worship itself—that is, to chop wood and other needs, not for divinity—and Scripture says he must be redeemed, as taught in the school of R. Yishmael: since this one went and became a priest for star worship, I would say let a stone fall after the falling one—that is, he should have no redemption; Scripture says "there shall be redemption for him"; and behold there is a kal va'chomer here—for there, since he already became a priest for star worship, nevertheless Scripture warned with a positive commandment that they must redeem him—all the more here that he has not yet begun star worship at all, it is obvious we must be careful at least in sit and do not act and not do an act to bring them to star worship; certainly this is a matter that is not equal on the heart to leave it; and proof further from what the Gaon Mahar Yehuda Minz z"l wrote in responsum siman 5, and these are his words: and when I saw it is impossible to save two souls of Israel that they not go to bad culture Heaven forbid, I said to permit his fellow's wet-nurse etc.; and he brings proof from chapter HaSholeach (Gittin daf 38) "that woman whom people work with forbidden matters," Abbaye said: if not that Shmuel said whoever frees his slave transgresses a positive commandment etc.—see there; and if so, kal va'chomer here that one should not act when there is concern from it—as explained clearly one should not act according to this Hagahah even though it is halachah from a holy place, Baal HaAgudah z"l; and in our Agudah this is not found at all. And see what is written in this seif in the name of Rashal:`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|6|_":
    `And to expel his sons from the school etc. So wrote Ran in the name of Rav Paltoi Gaon; and Rashal wrote on this in the last chapter of HaGozel siman 13: Heaven forbid in what the world is sustained only by the breath of schoolchildren—to abolish them? Granted regarding circumcision, for the essence of the mitzvah depends on the father and it is his honor; and likewise to bury his dead is incumbent upon him not to leave overnight and it is his honor—they coerce him until he accepts repentance and then they circumcise and bury; but Torah study of children for which he has no compensation—and upon him it is said "if you abandon Me a day, two days I abandon you"—obviously there is no reason for this matter; also what he wrote to expel his wife from the synagogue is not clear—if he sinned, what did she sin etc.? I do not believe these two things came from the mouth of the Gaon—end of his words; and one should wonder at his wonder—for according to his view he should have wondered more that they not bury his dead—if he sinned, what did that soul sin that it should lie in disgrace? And as Tur wrote siman 341 that disgrace of all his life; and it seems to me this Gaon deals only with his small children who have no merit on their own yet but depend on their father's merit—those there is authority to withhold burial; but not adults who are fit to receive reward or punishment on their own; and for this reason the Gaon also gave authority to expel his sons from school—and that is the small ones; but regarding his wife, certainly there is no reason to disgrace her, for she rises with him and does not descend with him—as it seems to me:`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|13|_":
    `This that we say etc. This is Raavad's view in Asheri, Tur; and we will explain his words with Heaven's help; and it will be explained what Rama wrote "and within thirty days" etc. is not precise, even though it follows from Kontresim's words that Beit Yosef brought: "Rabbi Yehuda said: nidui is not less than thirty days"; and Raavad challenged from what is found in the Gemara that Abbaye holds that in monetary matters we say "totally forbidden and totally permitted"—meaning bet din can remove him from cherem even immediately if he appeases his litigant; but for cherem they require thirty days specifically; and Rif ruled not like Abbaye but even for cherem it suffices if he appeases his litigant even immediately; if so, with what does the baraita "nidui is not less than thirty days" deal? If he appeased his litigant—not immediately we release; and if he did not appease him—after thirty do we release? And one can say it deals when he appeased his litigant, and this is what it says: they do not place him in nidui initially for less than thirty days, and if he repented they release immediately etc.; or when he did not appease him, nevertheless after thirty days they release him since he already accepted his judgment—provided he comes to bet din and requests they release him etc.; and Tur and Shulchan Aruch ruled like both answers leniently—that is, if they appeased him they release even within thirty, and after thirty even if they did not appease but he requested from bet din to release. And behold on the second leniency Rambam disagrees and holds they never release until the sage whom he disgraced wishes, as Beit Yosef wrote; except that also on the first leniency Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Kontresim that Rosh disagrees and holds like Abbaye that for cherem they require specifically thirty days, and not like Rif; and Beit Yosef wondered—from where does the author of Kontres have to say thus; and I say that certainly that author of Kontres was not precise here—for in Rosh's responsum he ruled explicitly like Rif's words, that there is no distinction between monetary and cherem—it suffices if he appeases his litigant even immediately; and also in chapter Elu Megalchin he brings Raavad's words and did not disagree with them. How can it be said he disagrees with them? Therefore it is clear what Rama wrote "some say"—that is Rosh's view that within thirty appeasement does not help; and returning this is not corrected—for Rif, Rambam, Raavad, and Rosh all hold this matter, and none disagree on the first leniency we mentioned; and Beit Yosef wished to reconcile Kontres's view from what is found at the beginning of Elu Megalchin on the festival, and counts there the menudeh whom the sages released for him—meaning he could not shave before the festival because a menudeh is forbidden to shave; and they ask in Yerushalmi, which Rosh brought: "in what do we stand? If they released him before the festival he shaves—meaning why did he not shave before the festival? And if they did not release him before the festival, he should not shave—meaning he should not shave on the festival, for he was not compelled before the festival to appease his litigant so they release him; but when they released him before the festival and his thirty fell on the festival—for nidui is not less than thirty days—from this we learn Rosh who brought this Yerushalmi holds nidui is not less than thirty days, and not like Rif and Raavad who do not require thirty—and it is a matter the mind cannot bear, for Tur also wrote here like Rif and Raavad, and nevertheless wrote in Orach Chayyim siman 531 that specifically if his thirtieth day fell on the festival, as the Yerushalmi said nidui is not less than thirty—and likewise Rosh; and his words contradict each other in one chapter in Elu Megalchin—at the beginning of the chapter he brings this Yerushalmi, and immediately afterward wrote like Rif and Raavad; and Beit Yosef resolved there that Rosh explains the Yerushalmi that what Abbaye said regarding cherem they require thirty days means that within thirty days release is not applicable at all even for after thirty days; and on this Rif said the halachah is not thus, but at least the release within thirty days helps for after thirty days; but at least we require thirty days for everyone; and I wonder that the holy mouth would say thus—for Rif and Rosh brought proof the halachah is not like Abbaye from those incidents of Resh Lakish and Rav Huna who placed in cherem a woman who extracted mention of the Name from her mouth etc., and there they did not wait thirty days at all; and it seems the rabbi Beit Yosef did not examine this sufficiently. And for the Maharach z"l I saw he wrote in Orach Chayyim to resolve: in Orach Chayyim it deals when he did not appease his litigant, only appeased bet din to remove him from cherem—what helps even within thirty days is that he be permitted after thirty days; therefore we require thirty days specifically, and after thirty days he shaves even on the festival; and this way is extremely wondrous—for if so, certainly forbidden to shave on the festival since it was possible for him to appease his litigant and then he would be permitted to shave immediately before the festival; and therefore forbidden to shave on the festival, as the Yerushalmi challenged; and we do not find we care about request from bet din except after thirty days since he already suffered his judgment, as Tur wrote here in the name of Raavad—but not within thirty days regarding that he can shave after thirty on the festival—this was not and was not created; and it seems to me thus: one should challenge the Yerushalmi and Tur who ruled like it that we require they release before the festival—what do we care about this, behold thirty days have not completed until the festival, and why should he be permitted to shave even if they released only on the festival? And why should he request release before the festival since nidui still continues upon him? And Tosafot beginning of Elu Megalchin also brought this Yerushalmi in this wording: Yerushalmi asks "behold he sinned that he did not come before the festival before the sages to release him"—and they answer that his thirty days of nidui completed on the festival, for nidui is not less than thirty days—end of his words; behold they did not require release before the festival in truth; and likewise proven in our Gemara in Elu Megalchin that Rava said: does it teach "that the sages released him"? "That the sages released for him" it teaches—that he goes and appeases his litigant; Rashi explained: on the festival the sages of that generation released for him; and if so, why did Tur rule they released him before the festival—behold our Gemara is primary; and further I can be precise in Tur's wording in Orach Chayyim, and these are his words: and likewise the menudeh who could not shave before—for behold a menudeh is forbidden to shave; and such as when they released him before the festival and the thirtieth day fell on the festival, for he could not shave before because nidui is not less than thirty days; but if he remained in nidui and did not request they release him, and on the festival requested they release him—he is not compelled and cannot shave—end of his words; it is difficult—for it implies that also in the latter case it is impossible to exit nidui before the festival like in the first case, except that in the latter case it deals that he did not request before; and why does he conclude "he is not compelled"—still he is compelled from the side that thirty days have not completed. Therefore it seems to me plainly that according to everyone the baraita "nidui is not less than thirty days" is explained in two ways—that is, if the nidui depends on time, such as one cannot appease because the sage whom he disgraced went away or other compulsion, or the sages saw fit to intensify upon him to be in nidui some time, as written below seif 40—in this we say simply nidui is not less than thirty days; but if it depends on appeasing the sage—then we do not care about thirty days, but initially at the time of nidui they say the nidui shall be thirty days, and when he repents they release immediately; and this itself is the dispute in the Yerushalmi—for the questioner holds nidui depends on appeasement, and on this he asks: why should he shave on the festival if they released him before he could shave then, and if they did not release before—from the side he did not appease it is difficult, for he sinned in not appeasing since the matter depends on appeasement; and they answer that this nidui does not depend on appeasement but on thirty days' time; and according to what I wrote, therefore even if they released him before the festival it is called compelled, and all the more if they did not release until the festival; and this is exactly Tur's words, and thus he says: and likewise the menudeh who could not shave etc.—meaning from the side he requires specifically thirty days and appeasement does not help, he shaves on the festival since his thirty fell on the festival; and what is written "and such as when they released on the festival and thirty fell" etc.—this word "and such as" does not refer to what is written "they released him before the festival," but to what follows "his thirty fell on the festival"—this specifically; but what is written "they released him before the festival" is not specifically but mentioned for amplification, as I explained in the Yerushalmi; and likewise wherever you find "they released before the festival" it is for amplification; and therefore Tur concludes properly: but if he remained in nidui and did not request—meaning but if the nidui depends only on appeasement and he remained in nidui and did not request to appease before the festival—then certainly he is not compelled; and it is found all sides are equal that where it depends on appeasement, appeasement suffices even immediately; and all the people to their place shall come in peace; and Rama's words here that within thirty days repentance does not help—it is not like anyone, and none is concerned for it—as it seems to me:`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|28|_":
    `If he treated lightly etc. In siman 228 seif 3 he ruled the opposite—see what is written there:`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|29|_":
    `Except in his presence. Ran wrote the reason is because of suspicion—for sometimes they release him and the menudeh does not know, and when they do not treat him with nidui they come to say they treat nidui lightly and he will not fear sages' nidui afterward; but when they placed him in nidui not in his presence [he may say: as they placed him in nidui not in his presence, so they released not in his presence]—end of his words; and from this we learn the sexton should proclaim so-and-so is clear of cherem also because of suspicion lest one exempt from cherem not hear—as explained:`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|33|_":
    `In the manner of an oath. Such as one who says "I swear to be in nidui if I do such-and-such." Likewise Beit Yosef; and what is written "and release ceremony is required," Beit Yosef wrote much: at the time of release they say because so-and-so was placed in nidui in his dream, he shall be permitted, suffering and curse shall not come upon him; and they say "long live Reuven and he shall not die" etc., "teach me the path of life" etc., "Hashem I heard Your report" etc.; and one says three times "and pass over" etc.—end of his words:`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|38|_":
    `In another matter etc. In Tur it is written "and Rambam wrote" etc.; and it is difficult: what does he disagree with Semag? And it seems Semag cited specifically unintentional—that he does not know the prohibition—it implies that if he knows but unintentionally it slipped from his mouth, one should place him in nidui; and from Rambam's words it implies every unintentional—for he wrote Scripture did not punish unintentional etc.; also the wording "warn him" implies it deals with other unintentional as well—for otherwise it should have said "only inform him":`,

  "siman_334/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|43|ג":
    `One who places in nidui on one not liable for nidui. Hagahot Maimoniyot: specifically when he knows he is not liable for nidui—for that which R. Eliezer said in Moed Katan; but one who placed in nidui who thinks this one is liable for nidui and erred—one should not place the one who placed in nidui in nidui; and Rashal wrote: some say specifically if that one whom he placed in nidui improperly is a Torah scholar; but not regarding an am ha'aretz; and the one who placed in nidui is a Torah scholar—and from that incident of R. Eliezer there is no proof, for perhaps that one whom R. Eliezer placed in nidui was greater than him:`,

  // ─── yad-avraham ───
  "siman_334/yad-avraham/part-001.txt|1|_":
    `(Siman 334 seif 43) One who treats lightly one matter of words of scribes. See in Shach s.k. 70 who wrote in the name of Ran to distinguish between a matter whose essence is rabbinic and a matter whose essence is from Torah. And see in Peri Chadash Hilchot Yom Tov siman 496 and Korban Netanel beginning of chapter Makom SheNahagu who wrote to reject Ran's distinction. And I settled his words with Hashem's help in my composition on Rambam Hilchot Talmud Torah chapter 6 halachah 14:`,
};
